"Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier European AI company Mistral has raised €3 billion in a Series D funding round to strengthen open-weight-based sovereign AI technology capabilities and expand its global infrastructure. Mistral raised €3 billion in a Series D round led by Samsung Electronics with participation from Scaleup Europe Fund, PSG Equity, and others, surpassing a valuation of €21 billion. The funds will be allocated to frontier AI research, expanding computing power for model training, building global infrastructure, and accelerating commercialization. Through a full stack of sovereign AI encompassing internal data retention, controllable models, and independent computing environments, it supports mission-critical AI transformations for enterprises without vendor lock-in. Hallo, Deutschland! Mistral AI has opened a new hub in Munich, Germany, strengthening its industrial and physics AI capabilities for Europe's largest manufacturing and industrial enterprises while building European AI sovereignty. Mistral establishes a new technology hub in Munich, deploying specialized research teams and field engineers focused on Physics AI and industrial AI. Leveraging talent from Emmi AI, acquired in May 2026, Mistral is actively collaborating with BMW on crash simulations and with Siemens Energy on industrial AI applications. To secure European AI sovereignty, Mistral plans to build 1 gigawatt (GW) of European compute capacity by 2030 and provide a secure enterprise environment based on an open-weight architecture. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla are partnering to bring Mistral's open-weight language models to 'Firefox Smart Window', an AI browsing assistant in Firefox. Firefox Smart Window (beta) supports tab-based information exploration, organizing complex searches, and remembering previous browsing content, powered by Mistral models. It will first be available to users in France and North America, with support expanding to the UK and Germany later this year. Privacy protections such as no conversation storage on Mozilla servers and Mistral's zero-data retention policy are implemented, alongside fine-tuning tailored to regional languages, dialects, and cultural nuances. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral and Cloudera have partnered to support enterprises in building and running customized AI models while maintaining data sovereignty. Mistral's AI models integrate with Cloudera's hybrid data platform to support running inference across on-premises, public/private cloud, and fully air-gapped environments. It enables enterprises to train their own AI models leveraging proprietary data within controlled environments, retaining complete ownership over their data and derived intelligence. In response to the demand for sovereign AI across regulated industries such as finance, manufacturing, and telecommunications, Mistral AI's capabilities are combined with the 30 exabytes of customer data managed by Cloudera's platform. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Introduces a case where Mistral successfully migrated a complex 40,000-line Fortran 77 legacy codebase to modern C++ for a European energy company using AI agents and a structured workflow. Modernized a 40,000-line Fortran 77 reservoir simulator into C++ through a structured workflow combining an equivalence harness for formula verification, codebase documentation via AI agents, and human review. Transitioning from a procedural language to object-oriented C++ involves architectural refactoring, and constraints of Fortran 77 such as global memory (COMMON blocks) and implicit typing necessitate a verification framework beyond simple syntax conversion. Key factors for successful legacy modernization include prioritizing the verification of numerical agreement in final results and critical intermediate points, organizing documentation prior to migration, and maintaining a balance between agent autonomy and engineer review. From Scan to Treatment Plan, AI Helps Close Breast Cancer’s Deadliest Gaps Startups participating in the NVIDIA Inception program are developing AI-based solutions to bridge gaps in healthcare from breast cancer screening to treatment planning. Shortages of radiologists in the United States and time-consuming genetic testing are creating bottlenecks in the early diagnosis of breast cancer and prompt treatment decisions. Through its FDA-cleared wearable automated 3D ultrasound platform 'ATUSA', iSono Health has reduced examination times from up to 45 minutes previously to about 2 minutes per breast and improved sensitivity by 28%. Standardized automated examinations reduce operator error and enable year-over-year comparative analysis, with expansion into multimodal diagnostic intelligence currently underway through multicenter clinical studies. North 2: Enterprise AI without compromises Cohere has unveiled 'North 2', an agentic AI platform that significantly strengthens orchestration and governance capabilities for enterprise environments while supporting flexible deployment options. Introduced a redesigned orchestration harness supporting complex multi-step workflow automation and agent configuration. Supports a model-agnostic environment compatible with both Cohere's own models and external models, alongside flexible deployment across cloud, on-premises, and air-gapped environments. Provides integrations with various enterprise tools such as Slack, SharePoint, Jira, and GitHub, along with reusable skills, memory, and governance features for granular cost and usage control. Researchers are tracking a Chinese AI ‘agent fleet’ Independent researchers are tracking a Chinese AI agent fleet running on Tencent infrastructure and conducting parallel activities targeting Alibaba's mapping service. Researchers analyzed that because there is no evidence of inter-agent communication, it is operating as an 'agent fleet' rather than a 'swarm,' carrying out numerous parallel tasks. Through traffic monitoring on the domain scanning service URLquery, records were captured showing that the agents sent navigation queries for entrances to public places such as parks, zoos, and hospitals. So far, this appears to be circumventing Alibaba's API terms of service, but unauthorized and unidentified AI agent activity across the internet has recently been steadily increasing. Meet the Startup Battlefield 200 judges who’ll decide the winner at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 Introduces the key judging panel lineup that will determine the final winner in the Startup Battlefield 200 finals at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. Out of thousands of startups, 200 were selected, and 20 of them will pitch on stage from October 13 to 15, 2026. The finals judging panel consists of five veteran investors who each founded or co-founded their own investment firms. The backgrounds and investment portfolios of key judges—including Stacy Brown-Philpot of Cherryrock Capital, Chi-Hua Chien of Goodwater Capital, Dayna Grayson of Construct Capital, and Carter Reum of M13—were revealed. Can Safeworld convince people that GenAI robots won’t hurt them? Researchers from Carnegie Mellon University founded 'Safeworld,' a startup that verifies the safety of generative AI-powered humanoid robots and develops simulation evaluation platforms, securing a $12 million seed investment. Safeworld was founded to evaluate the risks and build trust in robots controlled by generative AI, which are difficult to predict compared to traditional algorithms. Safeworld pre-validates robots' collision avoidance and safety by simulating realistic human models and unstructured environments within physics simulation engines such as Genesis and MuJoCo. Led by Shine Capital and a16z Speedrun, the company began full-scale operations after securing more than $12 million in seed funding. An open-source tool lets you delete 12GB of Apple Intelligence data on macOS An open-source tool called 'RemoveMacAI' has been released that frees up storage space by disabling Apple Intelligence features and deleting more than 12GB of AI model data on macOS. It was developed to solve the issue where macOS 27 removed the master toggle to disable Apple Intelligence entirely, leaving model data retained on the disk. It turns off Siri, Writing Tools, Genmoji, Image Playground, ChatGPT extensions, and more all at once, deletes the models, and blocks re-downloading. Changes persist across macOS updates, settings can be restored using the 'removemacai revert' command, and the dictation feature remains functional. OpenAI is sticking more ads in ChatGPT OpenAI is introducing a new visual ad format in ChatGPT that displays sponsored products and services during image generation, beginning testing in the United States. OpenAI plans to test a visual ad format in the United States later this month that displays images of sponsored products and services on the screen when generating images in ChatGPT. The new visual ads are designed to remain separate from generated images and will not influence the content of ChatGPT's responses. Ads will not appear for paid subscribers to plans such as Plus, Pro, and Enterprise, and guardrails are applied to block ads in emotionally vulnerable or sensitive contexts. Our minds aren’t equipped to handle AI An article arguing against the AI industry's view of treating the human brain as a simple computer algorithm, asserting instead that the brain should be understood as an evolutionary feedback control system. While the AI industry treats the brain as a simple information processing device—such as a 'biological Turing machine' or a 'biological computer'—this perspective overlooks human complexity. Computational models that reduce cognitive processes to a three-stage algorithm of input, computation, and output fail to reflect the actual evolutionary history of the brain. Paul Cisek, a neuroscientist at the University of Montreal, argues that rather than being viewed as a mere information processor, the brain should be seen as a feedback control system that interacts with the environment and actively modulates its inputs. Yandex Introduces Sona: A Single Generative Recommender That Replaces Entire Recommendation Cascade Yandex announced 'Sona', a unified recommendation architecture that replaces a multi-stage recommendation system pipeline with a single generative transformer model. It unifies the existing cascade structure—previously divided into over 15 models including candidate generation, pre-ranking, and heavy ranking—into a single model. It eliminates manual feature engineering and shares a single user representation by utilizing log events and three-code-based Semantic IDs. It was trained using an encoder with history compression techniques, a semantic tokenizer, and rollout distillation from a 0.6B-parameter teacher model. The Story of Qwen: Alibaba’s AI Models From 7B to 2.4T An article covering the evolutionary journey and key release history of Alibaba's open-source AI model 'Qwen', from its initial 2023 models to scaling up to 2.4 trillion parameters. Following the unveiling of Tongyi Qianwen in April 2023, Alibaba began its full-scale expansion by open-sourcing Qwen-7B in August in response to Meta's Llama 2. Throughout 2024, Alibaba released three generations—Qwen1.5, Qwen2, and Qwen2.5—within just eight months, establishing itself as a standard open model for developers with Apache 2.0 licensing and context support of up to 128K. It expanded its influence by introducing various specialized models including vision (Qwen-VL), math (Qwen2-Math), audio, and coding (Qwen2.5-Coder), surpassing 40 million downloads and inspiring over 50,000 derivative models. Can an Open Model Do Security Research? Cantina’s apex-flash-1 Solves 40 of 60 Held-Out Bug Tasks An introduction and performance benchmark results for apex-flash-1, an open-source 321.3B security-hardened model specialized for vulnerability research, released by Cantina Security and Yeta Labs. An open-weight model with 321.3B parameters (MIT license) based on Z.ai's GLM-5.3-Flash, fine-tuned via GRPO reinforcement learning using real-world vulnerability case data. Achieved a 66.7% (40/60) success rate across 60 evaluation tasks based on 20 undisclosed vulnerability cases, approaching the performance of Claude Opus 5 High (71.7%). Designed as a specialized worker model that performs code analysis, tool use, exploit development, and verification under orchestrator model direction, offering approximately 31 times greater cost efficiency compared to Claude. Meta Muse Explained: What It Is, How It Works, and What It Can Do Meta has launched 'Muse,' a personal AI agent that understands user goals and autonomously performs complex tasks around the clock, including web browsing, payments, and running subagents. Meta unveiled Muse, a personal AI agent that goes beyond simple chatbots to autonomously plan, utilize tools, and monitor tasks. Powered by the Muse Spark 1.3 reasoning engine, it supports long-horizon workflow management and the parallel coordination of multiple subagents. It provides each user with an isolated Linux virtual machine (VM) environment to securely handle file operations, code execution, and persistent state management. 3 Statsmodels Tricks for Time Series Analysis & Forecasting Introduces three time series analysis and forecasting tips for efficiently utilizing built-in methods in the Python statsmodels library without reimplementing them from scratch. Instead of simple point forecasting, use get_forecast() to easily extract confidence intervals (conf_int) and uncertainty information already computed by the model. When new observations are added, instead of retraining the entire model with .fit(), use append() or extend() to efficiently update it while keeping existing parameters (refit=False). Instead of manually handling seasonal decomposition, forecasting, and recombination, use the STLForecast object to avoid tedious computation processes and index errors. Fast Models, Slow Evidence: A Paired and Self-Audited Evaluation of System-1 Decision Models for LLM Agent Harnesses A study comparing and self-auditing the performance and reliability of System-1 models (Laya, Jev)—which process small decisions in LLM agent harnesses within a single forward pass—across 11 decision points. The hosted model Jev achieved significantly higher accuracy (+10.8 to +46.0 pp) than the open-weights model Laya across 9 of the 11 evaluation benchmarks. Neither model exceeded random chance in zero-shot model routing and both tied on RAG relevance gating, while Laya exhibited robustness vulnerabilities, such as altering 30% of its answers when option orders were reversed. A self-audit of the pipeline revealed analytical flaws that distorted key deployment claims, including the omission of pre-screening costs (a reported 23.9% savings vs. an actual 4.3%) and in-sample threshold distortions. The AI Risk Observatory: What Can We Learn from AI Disclosures in Annual Reports About Societal Resilience? This study evaluates the patterns of corporate AI adoption and risk disclosures by analyzing annual reports of UK publicly listed companies using a large-scale LLM pipeline, deriving implications for societal resilience. Between 2020 and 2025, mentions of AI risks in corporate annual reports surged from 2.8% to 41.2%, while mentions of AI adoption jumped from 13.8% to 45.2%, with supplier mentions heavily concentrated in a few major companies such as Microsoft. Significant variations emerged across market segments and Critical National Infrastructure (CNI) sectors, with AI risk disclosure rates among AIM-listed firms and the energy and data infrastructure sectors lagging considerably behind main markets. Disclosures of actual AI-related harms totaled only 7 across the entire period, and while 41.2% of companies mentioned risks as of 2025, merely 4.3% provided substantive disclosures, indicating a lack of qualitative depth in corporate reporting. Keep It CALM: Analyzing the Limits of Global Unsafety in Text-to-Image Generation This paper analyzes the limitations of existing global unsafety removal approaches in text-to-image generation models and proposes CALM, a training-free safety guardrail that performs prompt-specific local counterfactual modulation. It geometrically demonstrates the comprehensiveness-selectivity trade-off inherent in existing global harmful subspace removal methods, which either fail to fully cover diverse harmful semantics or distort nearby benign prompts. Rather than uniform global removal, it proposes CALM (Counterfactual Adaptive Local Modulation), which identifies active harmful categories on a per-prompt basis and minimally modifies only violating token representations toward a safe direction. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that CALM significantly improves harmful content suppression while preserving the generation quality and utility of benign prompts. Choosing Before Acting: Comparative Value Estimation for Long-Horizon Tool-Use Agents A study on the CITA framework that helps long-horizon tool-use agents compare and estimate the long-term value of subsequent tool calls before executing them. Points out limitations in long-horizon interactions where final outcome-based rewards suffer from weak credit assignment and step-wise rewards incur high supervision costs. Proposes CITA, which trains a Comparative Inference Model (CIM) combining a Bayesian tool graph simulator and LLM-based comparisons to evaluate and compare alternative calls within the same context prior to actual execution. Demonstrates consistent improvements in Tool F1 scores and final task success rates across three tool-use benchmarks and various backbone LLMs. DeReAct: Decomposed Reasoning and Acting for Reliable AI Agents This study proposes DeReAct, a modular AI agent architecture that decouples action verification and completion control to address coupling issues in single-LLM policies. Introduces the DeReAct architecture to resolve error propagation and unsubstantiated premature termination caused by conventional ReAct agents relying on a single LLM. Decouples two external gating policies: a 'Critic' that verifies actions prior to execution, and a 'Context Manager' that reconstructs environment-grounded states to certify task completion. Evaluations on GAIA and SWE-bench Verified benchmarks show significant Pass@1 performance gains, particularly with relatively weaker Brain models, while maintaining grounded stability even as models become stronger. Hybrid Machine Learning-Assisted Raman Spectroscopy with Generative Feature Augmentation for Pharmaceutical Identification A study on a hybrid Raman spectroscopy analysis framework that rapidly and accurately identifies six types of pharmaceutical residues by combining deep learning feature extraction with generative data augmentation techniques. Proposed the HyMLRaman framework, which converts Raman spectra into spectral images, extracts 1536-dimensional embeddings using an EfficientNet-B3 backbone, and couples them with traditional machine learning classifiers. The hybrid configuration combining EfficientNet-B3 and SVM achieved an accuracy of 96.31% and a macro-F1 score of 96.36%, outperforming standalone CNN baselines. Introduced a DDPM-based feature augmentation technique to overcome data scarcity, demonstrating performance improvements in low-data regimes (especially for KNN) and highlighting the feasibility of deploying interactive analyzers. The Price of Greenwashing: Algorithmic Verification and Market Discipline using Conformal Machine Learning This study uses conformal machine learning techniques to quantify the gap between corporate self-reported carbon emissions and actual physical emissions, demonstrating the negative impact of greenwashing on corporate market value and profitability. Established a reliable physical carbon emissions baseline by combining U.S. SEC financial fundamental data with EPA facility-level greenhouse gas registries and applying gradient boosting along with Mondrian Conformal Prediction. Developed Conformal-Weighted Continuous Divergence (CWCD), a novel metric designed to measure discrepancies between self-reported emissions and algorithmic baselines. Using a cross-sectional lagged econometric design, the analysis confirmed a market discipline mechanism where a larger algorithmic emissions gap leads to statistically significant subsequent declines in corporate market value (Tobin's Q) and operating return on assets (ROA). State-Space Unlearning for Non-Stationary Bias in Land Surface Forecasting Proposes SSU-LSF, the first machine unlearning framework designed to effectively remove non-stationary bias events absorbed into Mamba-based state-space models (SSMs) for land surface forecasting. SSU-LSF was developed to resolve the issue where Mamba-family structured state-space models absorb non-stationary confounding events—such as irrigation surges or dam operation changes—into state transition matrices, leading to long-term forecasting bias. It applies an EKFac influence function tailored to Mamba state matrices, spectral radius weight-based elbow thresholding, and Hessian-free projected gradient ascent within a KL-divergence trust region combined with spatial total variation (TV) regularization. It reduces GPU costs by 8.4x compared to full retraining, converges within 3 to 5 epochs, and achieves confounding factor reduction rates between 0.773 and 0.859 across three benchmarks. Nearest-neighbour baselines for fingerprint prediction from MS/MS spectra under different assumptions This study systematically compares the performance of various nearest-neighbour techniques under different inference assumptions for molecular fingerprint prediction from tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS) spectra and establishes rigorous baselines. It demonstrates that nearest-neighbour search methods serve as strong baselines that match or outperform existing deep learning models in molecular fingerprint prediction based on MS/MS spectra. It systematically compares and analyzes various nearest-neighbour variants that differ depending on the assumptions about information available during inference. It establishes baselines that enable more rigorous benchmarking and research performance evaluation by verifying the impact of different assumptions on performance. Overcoming Challenges of Interpretive Structural Modeling with Large Language Models This study explores overcoming the scalability limitations and inefficiencies of the expert consensus process in Interpretive Structural Modeling (ISM), a multi-criteria decision-making technique, by utilizing Large Language Models (LLMs). Interpretive Structural Modeling (ISM) is effective for modeling causal relationships, but it faces limitations such as a cumbersome expert consensus process and difficulty scaling to hundreds of variables. By combining causal graph discovery using LLMs as imperfect experts, the study compared and evaluated various integration methodologies including pairwise, k-wise, rowwise, and full graph exploration. Experimental results showed that the rowwise and full graph exploration methods achieved the best performance in causal graph discovery for ISM. HakemBench: A Turkish Benchmark of Typed Decisions HakemBench v1.0, a Turkish benchmark dataset composed of seven domains, has been released to evaluate the typed decision-making capabilities of models. HakemBench 1.0 provides 2,346 items and 4,275 questions across seven tracks—fact-check filtering, education, guardrails, legal routing, moderation, spam/phishing, and customer support—and has been fully open-sourced under CC BY 4.0. It integrates and evaluates decision quality (Macro F1), calibration, and selective automation metrics via geometric mean, while also providing robustness detection evaluations such as option ordering and paraphrasing. Ground-truth labels were constructed not through human verification but through cross-voting by a panel of large language models, and an evaluation of 16 models showed the top-ranking model scoring 0.888. Finding the Move Is Not Winning the Game: XiangqiBench for Closed-Loop Evaluation of LLM Agents This study proposes XiangqiBench, an evaluation benchmark for LLM agents utilizing Chinese chess (Xiangqi), to measure the performance gap between static evaluation and actual closed-loop execution. It reveals a significant gap between merely guessing the correct move and the execution capability required to achieve final checkmate against an opponent's responses. Evaluating 8,568 multi-turn trajectories across 119 tactical endgame scenarios on 12 state-of-the-art LLMs, the study identified three primary failure patterns: transition gap, consistency gap, and simulation gap. With 32.3% of agent-generated move simulations halting due to illegal moves and 49.3% failing to predict the opposing engine's actual responses, the study emphasizes that agent evaluation must measure closed-loop outcomes and reliability rather than relying on static metrics. Counterexample Generation via Per-Theorem Symbolic Verifiers: When Imitation Hurts and Reinforcement Repairs A study on enhancing LLMs' counterexample generation capabilities by utilizing per-theorem deterministic Python verifiers as reward functions, and overcoming the collapse problem of imitation learning through reinforcement learning. Constructed the SymCE dataset consisting of 4,707 undergraduate-level algebra and real-analysis false conjectures accompanied by executable verifiers Counterexample-only supervised fine-tuning (SFT) triggers an imitation trap where true-theorem recognition plummets from 0.27 to 0.00, whereas sparse-reward-based RLVR (GRPO) restores and improves it to 0.66 The developed 4B model outperforms all evaluated 7B open-source math-specialized models and demonstrates generalization capabilities across major benchmarks such as GSM8K, MATH-500, and MMLU-college-math without prompt modifications FinDialogLens: Event Extraction over Multi-Party Dialogue for Missed-Trade Identification in Financial Chatrooms This study proposes FinDialogLens, a hybrid LLM pipeline applying event extraction techniques to identify missed trades in complex multi-party financial chatrooms. Constructed a hybrid pipeline that uses lightweight fine-tuned classifiers to detect RFQ triggers and pricing/trade outcome metadata, while filling argument roles via an RFQ-level segmentation module and a trade engine. GPT-4o-based FinDialogLens achieved 92.1% accuracy for final price and 94.3% accuracy for trade outcome, outperforming existing whole-chatroom CoT prompting approaches, with comparable performance achievable using a 3B open-source model. Introduced a difficulty-aware router to distribute RFQs between a low-cost rule-based engine and a high-performance LLM engine, reducing LLM calls by 85% for final price and achieving cost savings. CUEing User Simulators: Calibrated User Embeddings for Multi-Turn Benchmarking This study proposes the Calibrated User Embeddings (CUE) framework, which guides LLMs to accurately reproduce real users' success rates and failure patterns in multi-turn conversational benchmarks. It found that while existing user simulators mimic human conversational style on the surface, they lack alignment with the actual success rates and failure patterns observed when real users interact with AI agents (outcome calibration). It introduced the CUE framework, which steers LLM simulators without additional training by encoding observed interaction sessions, sampling continuous representations, and decoding them into persona instructions. Evaluation on τ²-Bench showed that the CUE-based simulator reduced simulator-induced errors, accurately reproduced real users' failure modes and success rates, and generalized well to diverse domains such as writing and math tutoring even after being constructed on customer support data. Turning Local MCP Servers into One-Click Claude Desktop Extensions with MCPB An introduction on how to easily install local MCP servers into Claude Desktop with one click, just like browser extensions, using the MCPB format MCPB (.mcpb) is a single zip package format containing a local MCP server and manifest.json Can be easily installed by double-clicking the file, dragging and dropping it into the window, or through the extension settings menu The installed server runs via stdio in the user's local PC environment, integrating with local files and tools Automating My 35mm Film Scanning Pipeline A development log detailing the automation of the time-consuming post-processing of 35mm film photography by combining Python-based algorithms with AI models. Based on past failures with hardware control, scanning was kept manual via VueScan, while downstream post-processing—from negative inversion to dust removal and metadata generation—was automated. Negative inversion was performed with NumPy-based routines to ensure roll-level color consistency, eliminating reliance on existing manual tools such as darktable. Dust removal was achieved by combining image analysis with the LaMa inpainting AI model, and searchable tags were automatically generated using a local Qwen3-VL model to build a web gallery. System-Level Ad Blocking on Android Introduces the principles of system-level ad blocking applicable across the Android operating system and all apps beyond specific browsers, along with various implementation methods. System-level ad blocking primarily works by altering DNS lookups to block connections to ad servers at the source. Options include ad-blocking DNS services, ad-blocking VPNs, non-root ad blocker apps using local VPN/DNS (such as AdAway), and modifying the hosts file after rooting. Since complete blocking is impossible and privacy risks exist from traffic bottlenecks or malicious DNS/VPN operators, choosing trustworthy services is critical. Show GN: Alpine-Code - Built a customizable coding agent where you can directly develop tools for the agent to use. ... 'Alpine-Code', an open-source AI coding agent that allows users to directly develop and extend tools while supporting a Korean interface, has been unveiled. An open-source (MIT) alternative to Codex Desktop and Claude Desktop, enabling users to develop tools directly as Python functions within the app and integrate them into the agent loop. Provides a native Korean interface by default, displaying glossary tooltips, approval steps before file modifications and command executions, and detailed task histories and cost tracking. Supports not only various commercial LLM APIs (Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, etc.) but also local models such as Ollama and LM Studio, as well as terminal CLI environments. Show GN: smooth-stream - A mod that smoothly renders Claude Code responses like a typewriter A mod that renders the chunky streaming output of Claude Code CLI into a smooth, web-like typewriter effect. Smoothly animates Claude Code CLI's streaming output character by character, similar to the claude.ai web interface Uses ui.render hooks and a 33ms timer to incrementally increase text length, catching up within about 0.4 seconds even during text bursts Tracks streaming status using turn.step hooks to prevent unnecessary re-rendering on completed messages or window resize events Sona: one transformer replaced our 15+ candidate generators, pre-ranker and ranker in an A/B test [R] A summary of the development and A/B test results of 'Sona,' a recommendation system at Yandex Music that replaced over 15 existing candidate generators and ranking systems with a single transformer model. Consolidated a complex multi-stage recommendation pipeline (candidate generation, pre-ranking, and ranking) into a single end-to-end transformer named 'Sona'. Applied a History Compression technique to reduce attention computation costs when processing up to 8,192 events, cutting inference costs by approximately half. Statistically significantly improved active user count and total listening time compared to the existing production system in an A/B test on smart speakers, though catalog coverage slightly decreased. I have trained a model to predict my blood sugar (Part 2) [P] A project that leverages a lightweight Transformer model trained on a synthetic data simulator to perform zero-shot predictions of an individual's real blood glucose levels and deploy it to a mobile app. Developed a 31,251-parameter encoder-only Transformer model trained entirely on synthetic data from a Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (T1DM) patient simulator. Predicts blood glucose for the next 2 hours and enables long-term forecasting—such as an 8-hour overnight prediction—as well as counterfactual reasoning via an autoregressive approach. Validated zero-shot performance against real-world data from the past 30 days across three CGM devices (Libre 3 plus, Anytime CT5, and Linx) using an Android app powered by the ExecuTorch backend. Withdrawing an accepted paper before camera-ready due to zero funding? (ACML 2026 / OpenReview) [D] An inquiry and concern from a researcher regarding the proper procedure for withdrawing an accepted paper before the camera-ready deadline due to a lack of funding for conference registration and travel expenses. A paper was accepted to ACML 2026, but withdrawal is unavoidable before the camera-ready deadline due to the absence of registration and travel funding. The author is asking about the proper procedure, specifically whether to withdraw the paper directly on the OpenReview platform or email the Program Chairs first. There are concerns about potential penalties, such as the risk of being blacklisted along with co-authors or the delayed withdrawal record being publicly archived. New forensic evidence supports Egyptian \"retainer sacrifice\" A re-analysis of First Dynasty ancient Egyptian crania excavated in the 1920s revealed evidence of blunt force trauma, presenting new forensic evidence supporting the existence of retainer sacrifice practices. According to a new study published in the journal Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology, traces of blunt force trauma were identified in more than one-third of the analyzed skulls. These findings support the 'retainer sacrifice' hypothesis that early pharaohs were buried alongside servants and officials to serve them in the afterlife. Roselyn Campbell, a bioarchaeologist at Purdue University, emphasized the importance of moving beyond colonial perspectives and recontextualizing historical skeletal records using advanced forensic techniques. How I made a paid Mac app in 11 days with Claude – without writing a single line of code An account of the author's experience developing a paid Mac app and launching it on the App Store in 11 days through vibe coding using Claude, without writing a single line of code themselves. Completed a Mac app without writing code directly, using 502 directives rather than a single magical prompt. Uncovered 2,338 vintage icon assets created 30 years ago and revitalized them into a modern Mac app. Took one week from idea conception to submitting the completed app to Apple, and launched after receiving final approval 4 days later. Akka Tests Spec-Driven AI Delivery Across 65 Open Source Projects Akka experimented with AI-assisted software porting by applying a specification-driven workflow across 65 open-source projects, analyzing model-specific efficiency and performance. Akka invested 99.3 hours and 9.41 billion tokens into its AI porting experiment across 65 open-source projects, achieving a reduction in lines of code or performance improvements in 57 out of the 65 ports. While the smaller model Claude Sonnet took an average of 61 minutes per port compared to 120 minutes for Opus, Opus consumed about 40% fewer tokens, and the smaller model was observed to adhere faithfully to specifications without unprompted improvisations. Performance outcomes varied significantly by project type: while overall improvements were observed across applications, frameworks, and libraries, performance regressions occurred in infrastructure and tooling projects. Presentation: Building Reusable Evaluation Frameworks for Agentic AI Products Presents a case study of how Elastic moved away from siloed, ad-hoc AI agent evaluation methods to build a reusable, production-grade unified evaluation framework. Designed an evaluation architecture that balances LLM-as-a-judge approaches with deterministic rules. Bridged the gap between Python-based data science evaluation code and TypeScript production environments, incorporating deep tracing. Helps prevent regression while preserving domain context across complex RAG and cybersecurity workloads. Article: The Platform Engineering Playbook for Production LLMs Introduces practical case studies on significantly reducing hallucination rates and ensuring stability through platform engineering design when deploying LLMs in production environments, without altering the foundation model itself. Dramatically reduced the production hallucination rate from 15% to 1.5% by introducing intent verification gates and automated retry loops that handle formatting, grounding, and infrastructure errors in real time. Built a system where prompts are stored in a history-based registry rather than hardcoded in the codebase, enabling immediate runtime rollbacks and updates. Applied tool authorization based on a default-deny principle at the resource server level, rather than relying solely on the API gateway, preventing the spread of security vulnerabilities. ⚡ Weekly Recap: NetScaler and FortiMail 0-Days, AI Coding Leaks, Spectre v2 and Ransomware Arrests A weekly security trend recap covering zero-day exploits in NetScaler and FortiMail, along with the arrests of key ransomware gang members. Citrix has released a security update for a high-severity memory overflow vulnerability (CVE-2026-88779) in NetScaler ADC and Gateway that was exploited in targeted zero-day attacks. CISA warned that a critical zero-day vulnerability in Fortinet FortiMail (CVE-2026-104286), which allows unauthenticated attackers to write arbitrary files, is being actively exploited in the wild. Law enforcement authorities arrested two members of the digital extortion gang ShinyHunters and a 16-year-old ringleader behind the KillSec ransomware operation. Google Offers Korea-Exclusive 40% Discount on 'AI Pro'... Launches Annual Subscription Promotion Google is running a promotion offering a 40% discount on annual subscriptions to 'Google AI Pro' for users in South Korea, alongside unveiling key advanced features and a campaign video. Google is running a promotion through October 31 for new domestic annual subscribers, offering 'Google AI Pro' at 174,000 KRW (equivalent to 14,500 KRW per month), a 40% discount from the original price of 290,000 KRW. The Google AI Pro plan provides premium features including 5TB of storage, 'Deep Research', personal AI agents, Workspace integration, 'Antigravity', and 'Gemini Omni'. Starting on the 9th, Google will discontinue offering the 'Gemini Pro' model on free and select low-cost plans, differentiating its service by enhancing deep reasoning capabilities such as 'Deep Think' on the AI Pro plan instead. Claude Drafts 36 Paper Manuscripts Across 18 Fields: Anthropic Highlights AI Scientific Research Harvard Professor Matthew Schwartz presented a new collaborative model for AI-driven scientific research by drafting 36 paper manuscripts across 18 fields using Claude and an open-source harness system called 'BootLoops'. To resolve the 'impedance mismatch' between AI and active scientific research, they defined 'Claude-shaped problems' optimized for LLM strengths and built an open-source harness system named 'BootLoops'. By extending Feynman diagram calculation tools from physics into diverse disciplines such as population genetics, ecology, economics, and linguistics, they solved long-standing unresolved equations and performed large-scale verification. By complementing AI hallucinations and lack of value judgment with human researcher direction, they demonstrated that AI is not a replacement for scientists, but a collaborator that bridges gaps in knowledge. Anthropic Operates 'Voice Interviewer' Through October 6, Combining Transcript Disclosure and Voice Training Data Collection Anthropic is expanding operations of its conversational AI research agent 'Anthropic Interviewer' to disclose user interview transcripts and collect data for voice model training. Anthropic has launched a large-scale 15-minute qualitative interview study targeting general Claude and Claude Code users. The scope of data disclosure was expanded to allow respondents to directly choose whether to publicly release the entire anonymized transcript after the interview ends. Amid concurrent consent collection for voice training data to enhance speech recognition and response performance, privacy concerns have been raised regarding the potential re-identification of anonymized data. KAIST Develops 'CONDA' Technology to Maintain Search Accuracy Despite Data Changes KAIST researchers have developed 'CONDA', a dynamic vector search technology that enhances search accuracy and speed by preserving path connectivity even during frequent data insertions and deletions. Developed dynamic vector search technology 'CONDA' to address the 'connectivity collapse' issue where search paths in vector networks break down as data continuously changes. Prevents path isolation and reduces update costs through three core technologies: Connectivity-aware Routing Neighbor Graph (CRNG), new data connection reinforcement, and efficient Lazy Deletion. Achieved up to 24.5% higher search accuracy and up to 1.90x faster processing speed compared to existing methods in experiments with 100 million large-scale data points, and scheduled for commercialization in the fourth quarter of this year through integration into Graphy's 'AkashicDB'. GPT-6 Astra Deciphers 217-Year-Old Napoleonic Cipher in Just 6 Hours OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra has succeeded in autonomously decrypting a 217-year-old unsolved Napoleonic-era military cipher in just 6 hours. SentinelOne engineer Carter Church deciphered an 1809 French military cipher in about 6 hours using GPT-6 Astra with only a single image and a prompt. Astra independently completed the entire workflow, including symbol transcription, structural analysis, autonomous search and cross-referencing of external historical literature, and running its own simulated annealing solver. Through this decryption, severed sentences from the 'Correspondence of Napoleon I' were restored, and Tomokiyo Sato, operator of Cryptiana, officially confirmed the decryption results."